      {
        "id": "post-rpm-null-test-particle-action",
        "kind": "postulate",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Massless test-particle action and momentum scale",
        "statement": "Adopt a distinct massless test-particle sector on the prescribed background of [[post-rpm-test-clock-and-background]]. Its primitive action uses a positive einbein $e(s)$,\n\n$$S_0[x,e]=\\frac12\\int e^{-1}g(T,T)\\,ds,\\qquad T=dx/ds,$$\n\nand independent stationary variations in $x$ and $e$. The variational equations and their null branch follow from [[lem-rpm-einbein-variation-and-equivalence]]: the constraint is $g(T,T)=0$, and the physical branch requires $T$ to be nonzero and future-directed. The associated momentum $P=T/e$ is nonzero future null; its positive scale is primitive initial data, not $mU$ for any $m=0$ substitution. Null curves have no proper-time parameter. With $s$ in seconds, $T$ in metres per second, and $e$ in kg$^{-1}$, the integral has units joule-seconds. Under an increasing $C^1$ reparametrization $s=s(r)$, the paired transformation is $T_r=s' T$ and $e_r=s' e$, which leaves the functional invariant by [[lem-rpm-einbein-variation-and-equivalence]]; this is the formulation's gauge freedom. Neither the scale nor a clock reading is inferred from the ray image alone.\n\nThis is an adopted classical model formulation, not an empirical observation or a theorem. Its scope excludes mass generation, interactions, and quantum field effects.",
        "deps": [
          "post-rpm-test-clock-and-background",
          "lem-rpm-einbein-variation-and-equivalence"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "post-rpm-test-clock-and-background": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "lem-rpm-einbein-variation-and-equivalence": "formulation-prerequisite"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "dependency_level": 4,
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, String Theory, complete lecture notes, §1.1.2 relativistic particle and einbein",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/string/string1.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "Sean Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, complete lecture notes, Ch. 3 §3.3",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
            }
          ]
        },
        "physical_scope": "Separate classical massless test-particle sector with nonzero future-null momentum; no proper-time parameter, no P=mU identification, and no claim of empirical realization.",
        "library": "physics"
      },
      {
        "id": "def-rpm-mechanical-momentum-and-observer-energy",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Mechanical momentum and observer energy",
        "statement": "Use the massive and null sectors as separately adopted in [[post-rpm-massive-test-particle-action]] and [[post-rpm-null-test-particle-action]]. For a massive body of constant mass $m>0$, its **mechanical four-momentum** is $P=mU$, where $U$ is its future unit tangent with $g(U,U)=-c^2$. In the null sector, the mechanical four-momentum is the separately specified nonzero future-null vector $P$ from the einbein formulation; no null proper time or $P=mU$ is defined.",
        "deps": [
          "post-rpm-massive-test-particle-action",
          "post-rpm-null-test-particle-action",
          "def-gr-observer-and-rest-space",
          "lem-gr-observer-projection-decomposition"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
